TÚN ARTEFACT EXCEPTION
Version 1.1

This is an additional permission under section 7 of the GNU Affero General
Public License, version 3 ("AGPLv3"). It applies to any file that carries a
notice placed by the copyright holder stating that the file is governed by the
AGPLv3 together with this Exception. Those files are called the "Artefact
Runtime".

The Tún tools work by copying parts of the Artefact Runtime — markup, style
rules, scripts and templates — into the documents they produce. Without this
Exception, those copied parts could make each produced document a covered work.
The purpose of this Exception is to let people keep, publish, sell and share the
documents they produce under terms of their own choosing.

0. Definitions.

  A "Generated Artefact" is a document produced by a Tún tool that contains part
  or all of the Artefact Runtime together with content, data or text supplied by
  the person who ran the tool.

  A "Competing Generator" is a work whose purpose is to produce documents from
  data and templates in substantially the manner of the Tún tools.

  The "Origin Notice" is the machine-readable provenance block that a Tún tool
  writes into a Generated Artefact, in the part of the artefact that records how
  it was produced. It names Tún and states where the project can be found. It is
  a notice in the sense of section 7(b) of the AGPLv3.

1. Grant of additional permission.

  You have permission to convey a Generated Artefact under terms of your choice.
  The requirements of the AGPLv3 do not apply to a Generated Artefact, nor to the
  content, data or text that it carries, provided that:

    a) the Generated Artefact is not itself a Competing Generator; and

    b) the Origin Notice is preserved in the Generated Artefact.

  This permission applies however the Generated Artefact is delivered, including
  as a file, as an attachment, as an email, or as a page served over a network.

2. About the Origin Notice.

  Condition 1(b) is a requirement to preserve a notice, not a requirement to
  advertise. Specifically:

    a) The Origin Notice does not have to be visible to a reader of the
       Generated Artefact. Keeping it where the tool wrote it is enough. A
       Generated Artefact may be presented with no visible mention of Tún.

    b) Any visible "made with Tún" text that a Tún tool adds to a Generated
       Artefact is a default, not a condition. You may remove or replace it.

    c) You may translate or reformat the Origin Notice, and you may add to it,
       provided it still names Tún and still states where the project can be
       found.

    d) If a delivery format cannot carry the Origin Notice at all, condition
       1(b) does not apply to that delivery.

  You may not state or imply that a Generated Artefact was produced by something
  other than Tún, or that Tún endorses it.

3. What this Exception does not grant.

  This Exception does not grant permission to convey the Artefact Runtime itself,
  or a modified version of it, other than under the AGPLv3.

  Producing and serving Generated Artefacts is not, by itself, a modification of
  the covered work. Conveying a modified Artefact Runtime — for example shipping
  an altered template or an altered control script to someone else — remains
  subject to the AGPLv3 in full, including the obligation to offer corresponding
  source, and including section 13 where a modified version is made available to
  users over a network.

4. No other change.

  All other terms and conditions of the AGPLv3 remain in force. If any part of
  this Exception is held unenforceable, the AGPLv3 applies without it.
